<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="eumelanin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6498" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: CPD-12379 &quot;Eumelanin&quot;| is a common type of |FRAME: MELANIN| pigment, which is found in hair and skin. There are two types of eumelanin, black and brown, which differ by their pattern of polymer bonds. A small amount of black eumelanin in the absence of other pigments causes grey hair, while a small amount of brown eumelanin in the absence of other pigments causes yellow (blond) color hair. Spectroscopic studies of the biosynthesis of |FRAME: CPD-12379| showed that it takes place in three chromophoric phases. The first phase corresponds to the formation of the red pigment |FRAME: L-DOPACHROME| (&amp;lambda;max 475 nm) (see |FRAME: PWY-6481|). The second phase corresponds to a purple intermediate, which was designated |FRAME: CPD-12378|, with a broad absorption maximum at 540 nm, and the third phase is characterized by a general absorption due to |FRAME: CPD-12379| |CITS: [Napolitano85]|. The oxidation of |FRAME: L-DOPACHROME| to |FRAME: CPD-12378| occurs spontaneously &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; but has been shown to be under enzymatic control &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt;. |FRAME: L-DOPACHROME| is converted into two compounds - a spontaneous decarboxylation to |FRAME: DIHYDROXYINDOLE|, and an enzymatic tautomerization to |FRAME: 56-DIHYDROXYINDOLE-2-CARBOXYLATE| |CITS: [4031539]|. Both of these products are then oxidized to quinones, which are polymerized to form |FRAME: CPD-12378| and eventually |FRAME: CPD-12379|. In vertebrates, the pathway involves the activities of three related proteins, all members of the tyrosinase gene family: tyrosinase (|FRAME: HS01248|), tyrosinase-related protein 1 (|FRAME: G-11989|) and tyrosinase-related protein 2 (|FRAME: G-11991|). The enzymatic activities of |FRAME: HS01248-MONOMER| and tyrosinase-related protein 2 (|FRAME: CPLX-8053|) are well established. |FRAME: HS01248-MONOMER &quot;Tyrosinase&quot;| is the initial, rate-limiting enzyme of melanogenesis, catalyzing the first two steps from |FRAME: TYR| via |FRAME: L-DIHYDROXY-PHENYLALANINE| to |FRAME: DOPAQUINONE|, which goes through a series of spontaneous reactions yielding |FRAME: L-DOPACHROME| (see |FRAME: PWY-6481|). Subsequently, the |FRAME: G-11991|-encoded |FRAME: CPLX-8053| converts |FRAME: L-DOPACHROME| into |FRAME: 56-DIHYDROXYINDOLE-2-CARBOXYLATE| (DHICA). The role of the third family member, |FRAME: G-11989|, is less clear. Despite its structural similarity to Tyr and Dct, the enzymatic function of Tyrp1 is controversial and might be species-specific |CITS: [11775055]|. A |FRAME: MONOMER-15620| (DHICA oxidase) activity has been demonstrated for the murine |FRAME: G-11989| protein |CITS: [8027058][7813420][7855068]|, but in humans |FRAME: HS01248| and not TYRP1 performs this function |CITS: [9758418][11171088]|. As Tyr and Tyrp1 are part of the melanogenic complex and are known to form heterodimers, it is possible that the main function of human Tyrp1 is to stabilize tyrosinase |CITS: [7787879][9822646][18042623]|. The two products of |FRAME: L-DOPACHROME| oxidation, |FRAME: CPD-12373| and |FRAME: CPD-12374|, polymerize to form a mixture of different dimers and trimers, known under the name |FRAME: CPD-12378|. The polymerization can be induced by the presence of certain metal ions such as |FRAME: NI+2| or |FRAME: ZN+2| |CITS: [Napolitano85]|, but could also be catalyzed enzymatically by |FRAME: HS01248-MONOMER| or by peroxidases. Different routes were shown to result in the formation of different compounds. For example, studies using |FRAME: DIHYDROXYINDOLE| as substrate found that the predominant product was the 2-2&apos;-linked dimer |FRAME: CPD-12408| when metal ions were added, but the 2-4&apos;-linked dimer |FRAME: CPD-12409| when tyrosinase was added |CITS: [Napolitano85]|. When peroxidase was added to the reaction, the 2-4&apos;-linked dimer was still formed, but a third product, the 2-7&apos;-linked dimer |FRAME: CPD-12414| was also present |CITS: [Prota91]|. Similarly, when |FRAME: CPD-12374| was used as substrate, the predominant product was the 4-4&apos;-linked dimer |FRAME: CPD-12411| when metal ions were added, but the 4-7&apos;-linked dimer |FRAME: CPD-12412| when tyrosinase was added |CITS: [Prota91]|. Defects in TYR result in various forms of albinism. Mutations in TYRP1 in mice result in brown fur, but mutations in the human gene result in a form of oculocutaneous albinism (OCA3).</Description>
